	(text "ROVER BUILD ONLY.  The base populates DS1, R4-R9 and Q1-Q3, and nothing else on this sheet."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.24 33.02 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.905 1.905)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "5d1c0004-005b-4000-8000-00000000005b")
	)
	(text_box "WHY A DISPLAY AND NOT JUST A LAMP - #53 CANNOT BE SATISFIED BY THREE COLOURS\n\n#53 asks for two things a status LED can express and one it cannot.\n\n  IT CAN express three states - collecting FIX-quality epochs, collecting but degraded (FLOAT/SINGLE),\n  and not collecting at all. Three colours, done.\n\n  IT CAN express the coverage-hole distinction, which is the story's real payload: \"I could not drive\n  there\" versus \"I drove there and never got a FIX\". Those two holes need different remedies - a pole\n  in vertex mode versus coming back at a different time of day - and telling them apart at the desk\n  means a wasted trip. A lamp that says \"this patch is being covered at FIX quality\" already carries it.\n\n  IT CANNOT express the rule that the story spends a whole section on: NEVER SHOW A BINARY GOOD/BAD.\n  The property lines run through the tree line, and the tree line is exactly where the solution falls\n  over, so a green light meaning \"you are inside the boundary\" is least trustworthy precisely where the\n  operator most wants to trust it. What must be shown instead is a statement with its own uncertainty\n  attached - \"0.4 m inside, +/- 1.5 m, FLOAT\" - and, past a documented threshold, a REFUSAL to assert\n  at all. \"I don't know\" is a correct output and it has words in it.\n\nSo the indicator is a display AND a lamp AND a buzzer, each doing the thing it is good at:\n\n    LCD      the sentence, the numbers, the uncertainty, and the refusal.\n    RGB LED  the state, glanceable from arm's length with the operator's eyes on the ground ahead.\n    BUZZER   the transition, for the case where the operator is not looking at anything at all.\n\nTHE LCD IS ALSO WHY COLOUR IS NEVER THE ONLY CHANNEL. Red and green as the two ends of a status scale\nis the oldest accessibility failure there is. Every state the LED shows is written on the LCD in words\nat the same time, so the colour is a shortcut rather than the message."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 250 15.24 0)
		(size 124.5136 60.6072)
		(margins 1.27 1.27 1.27 1.27)
		(stroke
			(width 0.254)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 224 240 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "5d1c0004-005c-4000-8000-00000000005c")
	)
	(text_box "THE DISPLAY - A REFLECTIVE MEMORY LCD, AND THE FOUR THINGS ITS DATASHEET SAYS THAT WILL BITE\n\nCHOSEN: a Sharp LS027B7DH01 memory LCD, 2.7 in, 400 x 240, on the Adafruit breakout. It is REFLECTIVE -\n17.5 % reflection ratio, 14:1 contrast, no backlight (Sharp LCP-2110015A table 7-1) - so it works by\nreflecting ambient light and gets MORE readable as the sun gets brighter. That inverts the usual\nfailure: the display is at its best in the open field at midday and at its worst under heavy canopy at\ndusk, and the open field at midday is where the operator is.\n\n  UNDER CANOPY it is dimmer but still working - shade under pines at midday is still thousands of lux,\n  far more than a reflective LCD needs. AT DUSK OR IN THE DARK IT IS UNREADABLE, and that is accepted\n  rather than solved. Backlighting is not an option worth pursuing: the panel's transmission ratio is\n  0.25 %, so a 1000 cd/m2 backlight behind it yields about 2.5 cd/m2 at the surface. Front or edge\n  illumination is the only workable night fix, and it needs mechanical alignment that a schematic\n  cannot specify - the same reason due-blinky rejected an optical sensor. The RGB LED and the buzzer\n  remain, and a survey run in the dark is not a survey run.\n\nREJECTED: AN OLED. Bright, cheap, and completely washed out in direct sunlight, which is the single\nenvironment this device is guaranteed to be used in. REJECTED: A 16x2 CHARACTER LCD. Readable and\ncheap, but it cannot hold a coverage picture, and the coverage picture is what turns a 1.7-hour pass\ninto \"paint the property\" rather than a guess about whether the last row was captured.\n\n  THING ONE - IT IS A 5 V PANEL, ON A 3.3 V NODE. VDD and VDDA are 4.8 / 5.0 / 5.5 V (table 7-1); the\n  logic inputs are separately specified at 2.70 V minimum VIH with \"operation around 3V is recommended\".\n  THE BREAKOUT IS WHAT MAKES THIS WORK: it carries an AP2112K LDO for 3.3 V, an AP3602 CHARGE PUMP that\n  manufactures the panel's 5 V from VIN, and a 74HC4050 buffer running at 3.3 V so the panel always sees\n  3 V logic no matter what the header is driven with. VIN is therefore fed +3V3 here and the board makes\n  its own 5 V internally. Anyone re-drawing this around the bare panel needs a 5 V rail in the power\n  plan, which is exactly the kind of thing the hardware-first rule exists to surface before firmware.\n\n  THING TWO - ITS CHIP SELECT IS ACTIVE HIGH. Sharp's timing charts (sections 6-5-1 to 6-5-4) hold SCS\n  high for the whole transaction and low between, and table 6-3-2 specifies twSCSH, the SCS HIGH width,\n  as the active period - 220 us minimum in data-update mode. Section 6-5-1 adds \"When SCS becomes 'L',\n  M0 and M2 are cleared.\" Every other device on this SPI bus is active low, the card included. R11\n  (10 k to GND) holds SCS low so the display is DESELECTED while every Due pin is still an input out of\n  reset - the mirror image of R1 pulling SD_CS_N up on the node sheet. It also means SCS must be a plain\n  GPIO: a hardware chip-select peripheral produces the wrong polarity.\n\n  THING THREE - 2 MHz IS A CEILING, NOT A SUGGESTION, AND THE BUS IS SHARED. fSCLK is 1 MHz typical and\n  2 MHz maximum (table 6-3-1), and the panel is LSB-FIRST while the card is MSB-first. Firmware\n  reprograms both the SPI baud and the bit order on every switch between the two devices. Writing the\n  whole 12,000-byte framebuffer at 2 MHz takes about 50 ms, which is why the display is refreshed on\n  change rather than on every epoch.\n\n  THING FOUR - VCOM MUST KEEP INVERTING, AND IT MUST KEEP INVERTING WHEN THE FIRMWARE HAS STOPPED.\n  A memory LCD accumulates a latent charge in the liquid crystal if the common electrode is never\n  inverted; fCOM has a floor of 0.5 Hz (table 6-3-1) and Sharp's family application note asks that\n  intervals \"should not exceed one second\". There are two ways to do it: EXTMODE low and toggle the M1\n  flag inside each command (SOFTWARE), or EXTMODE high and toggle the EXTCOMIN pin (HARDWARE).\n\n     DECIDED: HARDWARE. EXTMODE (JP1-7) is strapped to +3V3, overriding the breakout's own 10 k\n     pull-down, and EXTCOMIN (JP1-9) is driven at about 1 Hz from D9. The breakout ships strapped for\n     software VCOM and the vendor driver toggles M1, so this is a deliberate departure - and the reason\n     is this project's own fault policy. The rover's safe state is TO STOP AND LEAVE THE FAULT ON THE\n     SCREEN. Software VCOM stops when the firmware stops, so the very state the display exists to hold\n     is the state in which the panel would sit un-inverted indefinitely. A timer output keeps toggling\n     through a halted CPU. It costs one wire, one strap and one timer channel.\n\n     Sharp also asks that a still image be refreshed at least every two hours (datasheet page 4), which\n     a halted rover cannot do - so the fault screen is a poor place to leave the device for a weekend,\n     and DISP (D8) is wired so firmware can blank the panel without losing its contents.\n\n  AND TWO MOUNTING CONSTRAINTS THAT ARE NOT ELECTRICAL BUT BELONG HERE ANYWAY. The datasheet, page 2:\n  \"Do not expose gate driver, etc. on the panel (circuit area outside display panel area) to light as it\n  may not operate properly. Design that shields gate driver, etc. from light is required when mounting\n  the LCD module.\" AND \"Do not leave the LCD panel in direct sun or under ultraviolet ray ... Liquid\n  crystal inside LCD panel deteriorates with ultraviolet ray.\" A sunlight-readable display that must be\n  shielded from sunlight around its border and aged by UV is a genuine tension: the enclosure bezel must\n  overlap the panel border, and the cart wants a cover for storage.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS IN RAM, WHICH IS A ROW IN #52'S BUDGET. The display is WRITE-ONLY - there is no way to\nread back what is on it - so the host holds the whole framebuffer: 400 x 240 bits = 96,000 bits =\n12,000 BYTES. (Adafruit's product page says 13.5 KB; the arithmetic and the vendor's own driver, which\nallocates WIDTH*HEIGHT/8, both say 12,000.) That is 12.5 % of the Due's 96 KB of SRAM and the largest\nsingle RAM consumer in the rover firmware - and it is why this display rules out the small AVR parts\nand does not rule out this board."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 250 80.9272 0)
		(size 124.5136 157.305)
		(margins 1.27 1.27 1.27 1.27)
		(stroke
			(width 0.254)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 222 245 222 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "5d1c0004-005d-4000-8000-00000000005d")
	)
	(text_box "THE RGB LED AND THE BUZZER - AND WHY NEITHER IS DRIVEN FROM A PIN\n\nNOT ONE MILLIAMP OF INDICATOR CURRENT COMES OUT OF A DUE PIN. Every channel is a low-side NPN switch\npulling from +5V, and the pin only ever drives a base resistor. There are two reasons, and the second\none is the one that forced it.\n\n  1. HEADROOM. A modern InGaN green or blue LED wants 3.0-3.2 V forward at a useful current. VOH on\n     this part is VDDIO - 0.4 V, so 2.90 V worst case (SAM3X table 45-2). Driven straight from a 3.3 V\n     pin, a green LED would be dim at best and dark at the worst corner - which is exactly the trap\n     due-blinky documented for its fault lamp. Off +5V there is 1.8-2.0 V to spend on a resistor and\n     the colour choice stops mattering.\n\n  2. CEILINGS. Ten milliamps per channel with all three lit is 30 mA. That is 200 % of the -15 mA\n     Group 1 per-pin limit if it came from one pin, and 23 % of the SAM3X's 130 mA TOTAL output current\n     for the whole 144-LQFP package (table 45-1) - a ceiling that is easy to forget because it is not\n     per pin. Through transistors, the package sees 3 x 0.62 mA of base drive.\n\nSIZING.\n\n    series, red     R4 = 270 R    (5.0 - 2.0 Vf - 0.2 Vce) / 270  =  10.4 mA\n    series, green   R5 = 150 R    (5.0 - 3.2 Vf - 0.2 Vce) / 150  =  10.7 mA\n    series, blue    R6 = 150 R    as green\n    base            R7-R9 = 3k3  (2.90 VOH - 0.85 Vbe) / 3300     =   0.62 mA\n                                 forced beta 17 against a minimum hFE of 70 at 10 mA - saturated with\n                                 a factor of four to spare, so Vce(sat) is well under the 0.2 V assumed.\n\nDEFAULT STATE IS DARK, AND IT HAS TO BE. Out of reset every Due PIO is an input with its pull-up on.\nAn NPN base fed through 3k3 from a 50-150 k internal pull-up sees at most 3.3 x 3.3/53 = 0.21 V, far\nbelow Vbe. So a board that has not run any firmware shows nothing, which means the indicator can never\nclaim a state it has not computed. That is the same rule due-blinky's fault lamp follows: every failure\nmode of the indicator lands on DARK, so you can lose the alarm and never gain a false one.\n\nTHE BUZZER IS FOR THE ONE MOMENT NOBODY IS LOOKING. The operator is watching the ground - roots,\nbriars, where the next wheel goes - not the display. The transition that matters is LOSING FIX, because\neverything driven after it is degraded, and it is precisely the transition a display cannot deliver to\nsomebody who is not looking at it. Q4 switches a self-drive magnetic buzzer from +5V; D1 is its\nfreewheel path, because a magnetic transducer is an inductor and switching one with a bare transistor\nputs the coil's collapse across the collector junction.\n\nTHE MARK BUTTON IS #53'S ACCEPTANCE TEST IN HARDWARE. The test is to walk or drive the tree line IN\nVERTEX MODE - stop, confirm, mark a point - which needs a way to say \"here\". SW1 is that. R12 pulls the\nline up so it is never floating, C9 gives it a 1 ms RC time constant against contact bounce, and R13\nis a 1 k series resistor between the node and the pin so that a firmware bug leaving PD7 configured as\nan output cannot short the rail through the button."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 250 243.3122 0)
		(size 124.5136 93.5256)
		(margins 1.27 1.27 1.27 1.27)
		(stroke
			(width 0.254)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 249 214 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "5d1c0004-005e-4000-8000-00000000005e")
	)
	(text_box "WHAT THE THREE COLOURS MEAN, AND WHAT THE FOURTH STATE IS\n\n  GREEN     collecting, FIX quality, and the ground under the cart is being covered.\n  AMBER     collecting, DEGRADED. Every epoch is still written - #52 drops nothing at write time,\n  (R+G)     because PPK reprocesses from raw observations and filtering during capture would throw\n            away the input to the process that fixes it. But nothing collected in this state counts\n            as covered ground.\n  RED       NOT COLLECTING. Storage full, card gone, receiver silent, or the supply is failing. This\n            is the rover's fault state and it is the reason the fault policy is \"stop and say so\"\n            rather than the base's \"reset and carry on\" - there is a human holding the handle, and\n            their catastrophic failure mode is walking two more hours of track while nothing is\n            written.\n  DARK      firmware is not running. Not a state the firmware can select, which is the point.\n\n  BLUE is not a fourth verdict. It is the mark-button acknowledgement - one blue flash means the vertex\n  went into the log - so that the operator gets feedback from something other than the display they are\n  not looking at.\n\nTHE HOLE CLASSIFICATION, WHICH IS THE WHOLE STORY. The LCD carries a coverage sketch of the property\nwith the current position on it, and each patch is one of three things: covered at FIX, TRAVERSED BUT\nNEVER FIXED, or never traversed. The second and third look identical at the desk and need completely\ndifferent remedies. The operator standing next to the hole already knows which one it is - they are the\none who turned around - and this display is what lets them record that knowledge instead of losing it."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 250 341.9178 0)
		(size 123.3452 50.3202)
		(margins 1.27 1.27 1.27 1.27)
		(stroke
			(width 0.254)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 214 214 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "5d1c0004-005f-4000-8000-00000000005f")
	)
	(embedded_fonts no)
